isplay":"−4.6","p":0.0001,"q":0.0001,"passes_fdr":true},{"month":10,"label":"Oct","name":"October","sen_per_decade":-4.59,"ci95":[-6.117,-2.905],"ci_text":"95% range −6.2 to −2.9","display":"−4.6","p":0.0001,"q":0.0001,"passes_fdr":true},{"month":11,"label":"Nov","name":"November","sen_per_decade":-3.87,"ci95":[-5.859,-2.08],"ci_text":"95% range −5.9 to −2.0","display":"−3.9","p":0.0006,"q":0.0007,"passes_fdr":true},{"month":12,"label":"Dec","name":"December","sen_per_decade":-4.51,"ci95":[-5.756,-3.236],"ci_text":"95% range −5.8 to −3.2","display":"−4.5","p":0.0,"q":0.0,"passes_fdr":true}],"extremes":null,"spatial":{"share_opposite_significant":0.0,"share_same_significant":0.668,"warn":false},"neighbour":null,"insights":[{"kind":"total_change","text":"Since 2003 the water stored on land got about 10.8 cm lower.","detail":"Total change −10.8 cm (95% range −13.1 to −8.4).","evidence_level":"strong","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"trend-test","numbers":{"total":-10.8}},{"kind":"signal_noise","text":"Since about 2007 the change has been bigger than a normal year-to-year swing.","evidence_level":"descriptive","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"signal-noise","numbers":{"year":2007,"ratio_now":7.32}},{"kind":"season_unresolved","text":"Summer shows the fastest loss of stored water, but the seasons are still too close to tell apart.","detail":"Summer: −5.5 cm per decade; the other seasons −4.9 to −4.0. Their 95% ranges overlap.","evidence_level":"descriptive","tested":false,"methods_anchor":"seasons","numbers":{"fast":-5.48}}],"insights_audit":{"tested":16,"passed":16,"shown":0,"q":0.05,"text":"Besides the main test, we ran 16 follow-up checks (12 months and 4 seasons). 16 held up after correcting for running many tests at once (false discovery rate q = 0.05); most of them repeat the main trend month by month. Findings marked “follow-up check” come from these checks."},"deeper_checks":null,"provenance":{"dataset":"NASA/DLR GRACE and NASA/GFZ GRACE-FO (JPL mascons) · lwe_thickness","version":"RL06.3Mv04","doi":"10.5067/TEMSC-3JC634","landing_page":"https://podaac.jpl.nasa.gov/dataset/TELLUS_GRAC-GRFO_MASCON_CRI_GRID_RL06.3_V4","accessed":"2026-11-14","processing":["converted longitudes 0..360 to -180..180","kept land cells only (product land_mask == 1)","country value = mean of the 2° map squares that overlap the country, weighted by the overlapping area (mainland and islands within about 1,000 km)","monthly series 2003-01 to 2025-12 (complete years)","anomalies from the calendar-month normal; Sen's slope, Mann-Kendall (Yue-Wang), OLS with effective sample size (trend_stats.py)"],"synthetic":false,"caveat":"Data: NASA/DLR GRACE and NASA/GFZ GRACE-FO satellites, which weigh the water on land by its gravity; JPL mascon solution RL06.3, blocks of about 300 km (averaged into 2° squares; coastal squares include some sea). The trend is a straight line through 2003–2025; it shows what changed, not why. The record is 23 years long, shorter than the 30 years usually used to describe a climate. GRACE weighs all water together (it cannot say whether groundwater, soil or snow changed), sees blocks of about 300 km, and has no data from mid-2017 to mid-2018 between the two missions; months without data are left out, never filled."}}